{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"532089c23e75e9ba","x":-700,"y":-2501,"width":2489,"height":1093,"type":"group","label":"Testing"},
		{"id":"2ab66381304891d0","type":"text","text":"Tutorial:\nLas funciones por el momento se obvian con la azúcar sintáctica de `let f params = body ;`. _\"Eso es una función `f` que recibe `params`\"_ es toda la información sobre funciones en esta etapa que el jugador necesita para resolver los ejercicios y se introducirá de forma paulatina, en particular al momento de usar operadores con ejercicios como _\"+1 al valor recibido\"_ y con condicionales para determinar que valor constante devolver (_\"si `true` devuelve 1, si `false` devuelve 0\"_ tipo de ejercicios)\n- Números\n- Booleanos\n- Operadores\n- Condicionales\n- Let & Let-Rec\n- Definiciones Top-level\n- Ejercicio final: una función que duplique elementos impares y que deje igual elementos pares","x":-160,"y":-375,"width":500,"height":515},
		{"id":"1bb9bf5908496011","type":"text","text":"Capitulo 1 - Lambdas y Estructuras\n- Tuplas como pares de datos \"congelados\", hay que \"sacar\" los elementos de la tupla primero y son fijas en largo.\n- Pattern Matching simple (primero con tuplas para \"rescatar\" un elemento de la tupla y operar sobre el, la idea es hacer andamiaje sobre la sintaxis de pattern matching)\n- Listas como un arreglo de datos \"operable\", son de largo dinámico por lo que no es necesario desarmar toda la lista antes de operar a diferencia de las tuplas\n- Operadores de listas (`:` y `++`)\n- Pattern Matching sobre listas (`head`, `tail`, la idea es introducir como se ve pattern matching sobre una lista)\n- Funciones recursivas con pattern matching\n- Funciones recursivas sobre listas\n- Lambdas\n- Ejercicio final: Recrear `len` o `map` usando pattern matching y funciones recursivas.","x":-160,"y":240,"width":500,"height":580},
		{"id":"28f9a93813302ad2","type":"text","text":"Capítulos","x":-600,"y":-265,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"2c3fd36df6f24a78","type":"text","text":"### Flow del Tutorial:\n\n+ Mover la cámara\n+ Se desbloquea hex de integer.\n\t+ Se conecta hex de integer al hex central\n+ Se desbloquean hexes de operadores binarios\n\t+ Se desbloquea al tiro un ejercicio por operador\n+ Al completar 2 ejercicios de operador binario, se desbloquean los hexes True y False, con un trabajo siendo entregar un booleano cualesquiera.\n\t+ Se desbloquean todos los operadores booleanos\n\t+ Al completar dos operaciones con operadores booleanos, se desbloquea el hex \"if\" y el hex de identificadores.\n+ Dos ejercicios de if, uno simple y otro anidado, donde el jugador aprende a usar los parámetros \"blanco\" y \"negro\" reservados por el editor.\n\t+ Al completar los ejercicios de \"if\" se desbloquea el hex \"let\"\n+ Un ejercicio simple de let. Se desbloquea hex let-rec\n+ Un ejercicio simple de let-rec (factorial?). Se desbloquean el hex de definiciones.\n+ El ejercicio final consiste en usar las definiciones top level que utilice multiples de los elementos vistos en la tutorial.","x":480,"y":-491,"width":489,"height":671},
		{"id":"36c5359531e19b48","type":"text","text":"Tutorial System\n- TutorialManager singleton, invoked at start and deleted  later if not needed\n- State Machine for tracking progress\n- Each state is a step in the tutorial","x":480,"y":-920,"width":250,"height":280},
		{"id":"1468b5f432c45c24","type":"text","text":"- Nombre\n- Disponibilidad\n- Género\n- Correo/contacto\n- Teléfono/Telegram","x":-680,"y":-2080,"width":250,"height":186},
		{"id":"84012f84edec9630","type":"text","text":"Protocolo Utility Testing","x":-400,"y":-2400,"width":250,"height":50},
		{"id":"45c7d61ca668964c","type":"text","text":"Post Inicial","x":-400,"y":-2220,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"63f7d3cb4d9d0a19","type":"text","text":"Cuestionario con preguntas en escala 1-10","x":-400,"y":-2080,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"072a0371b2f86898","type":"text","text":"- Nivel Ingles\n- Nivel Confianza en Programación\n- Nivel Familiaridad Programación Funcional\n- Familiaridad con juegos Automatización o similares\n- Han usado programación en bloques antes (Scratch, Game Builder Garage, etc.)","x":-400,"y":-1989,"width":250,"height":389},
		{"id":"b69ae811f9afc849","type":"text","text":"Introducción a Prueba Utilidad","x":-120,"y":-2220,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0d02b42a627349e3","type":"text","text":"Debrief de la entrevista y consentimiento del participante","x":-120,"y":-2080,"width":250,"height":100},
		{"id":"babe1f0bfc111884","type":"text","text":"- Describir en que consiste la prueba de utilidad\n\t- Que su voz y acciones en pantalla serán grabados junto las observaciones anotadas durante la prueba\n\t- Tienen que resolver la tutorial y completar 3 a 4 ejercicios adicionales usando la herramienta\n\t- Comentar en vivo durante la actividad que opinan de los distintos elementos de la interfaz, con un enfoque en claridad de los distintos elementos\n- Obtener el consentimiento del estudiante de forma verbal para participar en la prueba\n- Empezar con la prueba en el formato Think Aloud","x":-120,"y":-1894,"width":420,"height":466},
		{"id":"a8ca4ca73d3e2019","type":"text","text":"- Intuitivo\n- Tamaño texto\n- Font\n- Sistema de \"Descender\"\n\t- Intuitivo Mecánicamente\n\t- Intuitivo como metáfora\n- Sistema de \"Identifiers\"\n\t- Si queda claro que son análogos a variables\n\t- Cantidad de variables disponibles\n\t- Como funciona scope y shadowing\n- Sistema de \"Jobs\"\n\t- Si prefieren un sistema lineal o un sistema con un \"pool\" de trabajos\n\t- Desbloqueo directo o abstracción mediante un sistema intermediario (\"renown\")\n- Canvas\n\t- Si se encontraron limitados por la grilla hexagonal\n\t- Si los controles para configurar y colocar hexes resulta simple\n- Blueprints\n\t- Si ven valor en el sistema de blueprints y si lo usarían en sesiones más extensas\n- ","x":320,"y":-2110,"width":580,"height":682},
		{"id":"ed54a62b3104c09d","type":"text","text":"Entrevista Final","x":320,"y":-2220,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"cdb6cb70c054807d","type":"text","text":"Durante Prueba:\n- Tomar nota de puntos de fricción","x":470,"y":-2481,"width":860,"height":213},
		{"id":"de160ea261e2636b","type":"text","text":"Here's the curated instrument, tailored for **FP newcomers**, focused on **usability/friction**, structured as a **moderated session**. Participant-facing wording stays jargon-free; the technical notes are for you/the moderator. Budget ~45–60 min per session.\n\n## 1. Session flow\n\n1. Intro + consent + \"think aloud\" instruction (2 min)\n2. Background questions (3 min)\n3. Tutorial observation (let them do the built-in tutorial, observe only) (~10 min)\n4. 3 task scenarios, think-aloud (20–25 min)\n5. Post-session SUS + friction items (5 min)\n6. Interview (10 min)\n\n**Golden rule for FP-newcomer friction studies:** don't teach. When they get stuck, ask \"What do you think you'd do next?\" and let the silence sit. The dead-ends _are_ the data. Only rescue after they're truly blocked (note it as a \"critical incident\").\n\n## 2. Background (pre-session)\n\n- How would you rate your programming experience? (none / some / comfortable)\n- Have you used any visual / block-based coding tool before (Scratch, node editors, etc.)?\n- Have you heard of \"functional programming\"? (no / heard of it / used it)\n\nThese don't score usability — they let you segment friction by prior exposure.\n\n## 3. Task scenarios (think-aloud)\n\nFor each: record **success** (complete / partial / failed), **time on task**, **# of dead-ends**, **# help requests**, **where they hesitated**, and a verbatim quote. End each task with the **Single Ease Question (SEQ)**: _\"How easy or difficult was that task?\"_ (1 = very difficult … 7 = very easy).\n\n**Task 1 — Place & set a value.** \"Put a number into your program and make it come out as the result.\"  \n→ Watches: finding the Integer category, placing a hex, opening the config panel, typing a value, wiring into the Do hex. _This is your tutorial-transfer check._\n\n**Task 2 — Combine two things.** \"Make the program add two numbers together.\"  \n→ Watches: finding the operator, wiring two inputs, edge input/output legibility, whether they grasp the Do hex as the output.\n\n**Task 3 — Recover from a mistake (seeded).** Hand them a half-built/broken program. \"This program isn't working — can you figure out what's wrong and fix it?\"  \n→ Watches: do the validation/error messages and the output panel actually guide them? Can they undo/rewire? _Highest-signal friction task._\n\n(Optional Task 4 if energy is high: \"Make the program produce a list of three numbers\" — tests descending into an inner level and getting back out.)\n\n## 4. Observer friction log (per task)\n\nTally these signals — they're where moderated studies beat surveys:\n\n- Hunted for a hex/control >10s\n- Wired the wrong edge or wrong direction\n- Lost track of which level/context they were editing\n- Misread a glyph/color/label\n- Re-read an error message without it helping\n- Said some variant of \"wait, what just happened?\"\n- Worked _around_ a feature instead of using it\n\n## 5. Post-session questionnaire\n\n**SUS backbone** — the standard 10 alternating items (keep them verbatim so the score stays comparable across rounds). Then these **tailored friction items** (1–5, agree/disagree):\n\n- I could usually find the piece I needed without hunting for it.\n- Connecting pieces together worked the way I expected.\n- I could tell which connection points were inputs and which were outputs.\n- I always knew which part of the program I was currently editing.\n- When something was wrong, the tool helped me understand what.\n- Moving around the canvas was comfortable.\n- It was easy to undo or change something I'd done.\n- When I got stuck, I could usually figure out what to try next.\n\n## 6. Interview guide (open-ended)\n\n- Walk me through the moment you felt most stuck today. What was happening?\n- Was there anything that behaved differently than you expected?\n- _(Show the Do hex.)_ In your own words, what does this piece do? _(Probes whether the core \"output\" mental model formed — a usability tell even though it sounds conceptual.)_\n- What did the colors on the identifier pieces mean to you?\n- If you could change one thing to make this less frustrating, what would it be?\n- Was there anything you wanted to do but couldn't find a way to?\n\n## Scoring/analysis notes\n\n- Report **SUS score** per round (track the trend; ~68 is the average baseline).\n- Report **task success rate** and **median SEQ** per task — a task with high self-reported ease but low completion is a \"false confidence\" red flag worth digging into.\n- Cluster the **critical incidents** (full blocks) by area (finding hexes / wiring / level navigation / error recovery) — that ranking is your fix backlog.\n\nIf you'd like, I can turn this into a printable moderator script + a tally sheet (markdown or a doc in the repo), or wire a short version of the post-session items into the in-app settings/feedback panel you have planned in the TODO.","x":969,"y":-2118,"width":800,"height":690}
	],
